code FLOODS the screen. Kade's body CONVULSES.\nHis eyes go wide. He collapses.\n```\n\n**Elevated Version:**\n```\nThe screen WHITES OUT—pure, blinding light.\n\nKade's reflection in the monitor FRACTURES like broken glass.\n\nHis hand SLAMS the keyboard—keys crack—but the code keeps flowing.\n\nCLOSE ON: His eyes—pupils dilating, then FLOODING with cascading green code.\n\nHis body goes rigid. Fingers frozen on keys.\n\nThe monitor shows his heartbeat flatline... but the code PULSES in rhythm with it, as if the screen is now breathing for him.\n\nHe collapses. The code stops.\n\nThe screen goes black.\n\nThen: ONE CURSOR BLINKS where his hand rests.\n```\n\n**Why This Works:**\n- Visual metaphor (reflection fracturing = identity shattering)\n- Sensory details (keys cracking, code pulsing)\n- Foreshadowing (cursor blink = he's already becoming digital)\n- More cinematic, less clinical\n\n**Apply This Thinking To:**\n- Scene 5: Kade's first ghost movements (show don't tell)\n- Scene 7: The corrupted ghost attack (needs more visceral action)\n- Scene 9: Café meeting (visual tension through environment)\n\n---\n\n### 🟡 **3. PACING - UNEVEN SCENE LENGTHS**\n\n**Problem:** Scene 4 (Maya's grief) and Scene 6 (laptop message) are too long for their narrative weight.\n\n**Current Scene 6 Breakdown:**\n- Maya enters apartment\n- Sees flowers/card\n- Cries\n- Laptop opens itself\n- Message appears\n- She responds\n- Kade explains\n- She asks questions\n- More explanation\n- Emotional exchange\n- Promise to meet\n\n**That's 11+ beats in one scene.** Too much. This should be 2-3 scenes.\n\n**Suggested Split:**\n\n**SCENE 6A: THE MESSAGE (2 minutes max)**\n- Maya enters\n- Laptop opens\n- \"MAYA\" appears\n- She RUNS from the apartment\n- End scene on her terror\n\n**SCENE 6B: THE PARKING LOT (1 minute)**\n- Maya in her car, hyperventilating\n- Phone buzzes: \"Please don't run. It's really me.\"\n- Photo evidence appears\n- She's torn between fear and hope\n- End scene on her decision to respond\n\n**SCENE 6C: THE CONVERSATION (3 minutes)**\n- Now the Q\u0026A happens\n- But it's briefer, more urgent\n- Ends with café meeting setup\n\n**Why This Works:**\n- Each scene has ONE emotional beat\n- Creates mini-cliffhangers\n- Allows audience to breathe between revelations\n- More cinematic rhythm\n\n---\n\n### 🔴 **4. STAKES CLARITY - VAGUE THREATS**\n\n**Problem:** We know Kade is in danger, but we don't know *specifically what happens if he fails.*\n\n**Current Voss Scene:**\n```\nVOSS: Fascinating. He's learning faster than the others.\nTECH: Should we deploy Hunters?\nVOSS: No. Let him run. We'll be ready to collect.\n```\n\n**This Tells Us:**\n- Voss is patient ✅\n- Kade is special ✅\n- Hunters exist ✅\n\n**This DOESN'T Tell Us:**\n- What does \"collect\" mean?\n- What happened to \"the others\"?\n- What's the consequence of capture?\n\n**Refinement Suggestion:**\n\n```\nVOSS: Fascinating. Combat adaptation in under an hour.\n      The others took weeks before fragmentation.\n\nTECH: Should we deploy Hunters?\n\nVOSS: No. Hunters would damage him. I want this one intact.\n      (studies screen)\n      Besides, he'll come to us. They always do when \n      they realize what they're becoming.\n\nTECH: And if he doesn't fragment first?\n\nVOSS: Then we'll have our first stable weapon.\n```\n\n**Now We Know:**\n- Fragmentation is the natural threat (ticking clock)\n- Capture means weaponization (specific fate)\n- Kade is racing against his own decay (internal + external conflict)\n\n**Apply This Thinking:**\n- Every threat needs a specific consequence\n- Every deadline needs a \"what happens if\" answer\n- Vague danger is less compelling than specific horror\n\n---\n\n## STRUCTURAL NOTES\n\n### ✅ **WHAT'S WORKING:**\n\n1. **Cold Open Structure** - Starting with the code discovery is correct\n2. **Death Placement** - Scene 3 is the right timing (not too early, not too late)\n3. **Antagonist Introduction** - Voss appearing in Scene 10 maintains